# Engineering section guide

Use this guide for section 7, Engineering, of a feature's `{{vars.techDesignFile}}`: code
organization, testability, required test levels, pipeline gates and developer experience, as
requirements the code base must meet, not a plan of files and classes. Write only what differs
from the project baseline, `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/{{vars.techDesignFile}}`: a code screened as
Baseline gets no row here, and a refinement cites the baseline ID it refines.

## When and which codes

Testability is applicable by default, so consider this section on every task, but write rows
only for what the baseline does not already require (often nothing).

| Code | Covers here |
|---|---|
| MAINT | modularity (module boundaries, dependency direction), reusability, analysability (finding the cause of a failure), modifiability (changes that must stay cheap), testability |
| CICD | a pipeline gate the feature needs beyond the baseline's |
| DX | local setup, fakes and seed data for services the feature adds, time to a green test run |

Write each requirement once; elsewhere cite its ID.

## What to write

Rows in the [requirement format](../requirement-format.md). Refinements end the Requirement cell
with `(refines SYS-<CODE>-n)`.

- **Module boundaries:** which capability must not depend on which, in logical names (features
  from the index, glossary terms, roles such as "the payment provider's client"). Verify by
  `static` (an architecture or import rule) or `inspect`.
- **Modifiability:** the likely change the design must keep cheap, as a change scenario of the
  requirement format: the change, what it may touch, what it must not.
- **Testability:** what tests must be able to control or observe: time, randomness, external
  services replaced by fakes, seed data for every scenario's starting state.
- **Test levels:** which IDs need which level when the baseline does not settle it: rules at unit
  level, DCONS rows against the real store, section 4 contracts by contract tests, PERF rows by a
  bench, the main journey end to end.
- **Pipeline gates:** a new gate only (contract tests block merge, a bench runs on the main branch).
- **Developer experience:** running the feature locally with its new dependencies faked, time from
  clone to a green run, data to demonstrate the scenarios.

## Never write

- Folder, package, file, class or function names; design patterns to use; layering recipes.
- Frameworks, test runners, linters or versions, unless a constraint (`CON-n`).
- Coverage percentages that no gate checks; style rules the linter already enforces.
- A refactoring plan for today's code: a follow-up in the brief's hand-off.
- The list of tests this task adds: that is the implementation plan in the brief.

## Greenfield, adoption, brownfield

- **Greenfield:** the baseline sets the test levels, gates and local setup that the first
  implementation increment builds. Ask with proposed defaults (tests, type check, lint and
  dependency audit block merge; one command from clone to green tests); they become baseline rows
  with the user's consent. This section adds only feature-specific needs.
- **Adoption and brownfield:** for the touched area only, read lint and architecture rule
  configuration, the test setup and the levels it has, CI workflows and required checks, local
  setup scripts and docs, and how the touched code is organized today (to state boundaries in
  logical terms). Record the tools in use as `CON-n`; under adoption without a baseline, seed the
  baseline gates from the CI configuration with the user's consent. A test level the feature
  needs but the project lacks is a requirement, not a gap to accept.
- **Editing an existing section:** change rows in place, keep IDs, move removed or replaced rows to
  section 8, Retired IDs.

## Example

| ID | Sub-characteristic | Requirement | Measure | Verify by | Priority | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| CHK-MAINT-1 | Modularity | Checkout rules shall not depend on the payment provider's client | 0 such dependencies | static | Must | user |
| CHK-MAINT-2 | Testability | Rules that depend on dates (CHK-4) shall be testable at any date without waiting | tests set the date; no test waits for real time | inspect | Must | user |
| CHK-MAINT-3 | Modifiability | When a payment method is added, the change shall touch only the payment capability and its tests | 0 checkout rules changed | inspect | Should | user |
| CHK-CICD-1 | Gates | The order-placed contract test shall block merge when it fails (refines SYS-CICD-1) | required check | inspect | Must | user |
| CHK-DX-1 | Local setup | A developer shall run checkout locally with the payment provider faked | one command, ≤ 5 min from clone | inspect | Should | user |

## Staleness pitfalls

- Paths and type names in a boundary rule: use the logical names of capabilities.
- A tool version or a framework idiom: a constraint at most, never a requirement.
- "Currently untested", "needs cleanup": follow-ups in the brief's hand-off.
- Rows that hold for every feature: they belong to the baseline; propose moving them.
